We explain the effectiveness of SFT-as-context using a Bayesian framework for in-context learning ( Xie et al., 2022 ; Hu et al., 2024 ) , building on earlier Bayesian models ( Baum and Petrie, 1966 ; Blei et al., 2003 ) . Following Xie et al. (2022) , we model response generation as conditioned on a domain variable $	heta$ , with output distribution $\operatorname{Pr}(Y\mid x,	heta)$ . Each such variable $	heta$ corresponds to a domain such as nutrition estimation, math, instruction-following, and multilinguality. The parent model is trained using a large dataset across many different domains and therefore has many capabilities. Let $\Theta_{	ext{parent}}$ denote the parent model’s domain set. We assume that the SFT model is fine-tuned on a single domain $	heta_{	ext{SFT}}$ and denote its query set by $D_{	ext{SFT}}$ . Our analysis explains how SFT-as-context combines SFT expertise with the parent model’s general capabilities. For a query $x$ , let $\operatorname{Pr}_{	ext{parent}}(\cdot\mid x)$ and $\operatorname{Pr}_{	ext{SFT}}(\cdot\mid x)$ denote the parent and SFT response distributions, respectively, and define $\operatorname{Pr}_{	ext{SAC}}(\cdot\mid x):=\operatorname{Pr}_{	ext{parent}}(\cdot\mid x,y_{	ext{SFT}},I)$ , where $y_{	ext{SFT}}$ is the SFT response and $I$ denotes the SFT-as-context instructions. All variables are discrete. For each domain, assume an optimal domain variable $	heta^{*}$ inducing the optimal response law $\operatorname{Pr}_{*}(\cdot\mid x):=\operatorname{Pr}(\cdot\mid x,	heta^{*})$ . For $m\in\{	ext{parent},	ext{SFT},	ext{SAC}\}$ , define the corresponding errors $\mathcal{E}_{m}:=\operatorname{KL}\!\left(\operatorname{Pr}_{*}(\cdot\mid x)\,\|\,\operatorname{Pr}_{m}(\cdot\mid x)ight).$ We model the parent and SFT-as-context distributions as mixtures: $\displaystyle\operatorname{Pr}_{	ext{parent}}(y\mid x)$ $\displaystyle=\sum_{	heta\in\Theta_{	ext{parent}}}\operatorname{Pr}_{	ext{parent}}(y\mid x,	heta)\operatorname{Pr}_{	ext{parent}}(	heta\mid x),$ $\displaystyle\operatorname{Pr}_{	ext{SAC}}(y\mid x)$ $\displaystyle=\sum_{	heta\in\Theta_{	ext{parent}}}\operatorname{Pr}_{	ext{parent}}(y\mid x,	heta)\operatorname{Pr}_{	ext{SAC}}(	heta\mid x,y_{	ext{SFT}},I).$ Thus, conditioning on the SFT response and instructions changes the domain posterior while preserving the per-domain conditional output laws. We refer to $\operatorname{Pr}_{\cdot}(	heta|x)$ as the posterior probability, which under the Bayesian framework is the probability of the domain variable conditioned on the query. Intuitively, we model the LLM’s generation process as predicting a domain based on the query with some probability and then generating the response conditioned on the domain variable ( Xie et al., 2022 ) . We make the following assumptions for all $x$ where all logarithms are natural, and the total variation is defined as $\operatorname{TV}(P,Q):= rac{1}{2}\sum_{y}|P(y)-Q(y)|$ . We present assumptions intuitively followed by the formalism. 1. The posterior probability of the SFT domain variable is low for the parent model. Formally, $0<a_{x}:=\operatorname{Pr}_{	ext{parent}}(	heta_{	ext{SFT}}\mid x)\leq\epsilon$ for the input $x$ that belongs to the SFT domain, where $	heta_{	ext{SFT}}$ is the optimal domain variable for the SFT domain. 2. The posterior probability of the SFT domain variable given the SFT response is $\epsilon^{\prime}$ -close to 1. Formally, under the parent model, the SFT response $y_{	ext{SFT}}$ satisfies $\operatorname{Pr}_{	ext{SAC}}\!\left(	heta_{	ext{SFT}}\mid x,I,y_{	ext{SFT}}ight)\geq 1-\epsilon^{\prime}$ , meaning that the posterior probability assigned to the optimal domain variable $	heta_{	ext{SFT}}$ is within $\epsilon^{\prime}$ of 1. 3. The optimal response distribution is separated from that of the parent model by some margin ( Xie et al., 2022 ) . Let $Q_{	ext{parent}}$ denote the parent model’s response distribution conditioned on a non-SFT domain: $Q_{	ext{parent}}(y\mid x):=\operator